														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4861796">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I kinda like the core of this idea; the way the tattoo works and what it does, and the angle of it being a victim of a biker gang getting revenge and stuff works for me.</p>
<p>The narrative does not: it's perhaps a personal thing but I generally do not like when all of the central narrative in an article is backstory: my general preference is that the Foundation are the protagonists of the story since the article is written from their POV; and so everything has to be centred around what they know.</p>
<p>My other concern with it is that it feels very flat: there's no emotional highs or escalation or anything; so it just kinda feels like a sequence of events that happen without it feeling like it's building up to something or going somewhere; and so the ending feels very conclusive but almost unearned because there's not really any kinda sense of catharsis or w/e when it's finally contained.</p>
<p>So yeah: my advice is that this has elements which work, and it feels like a good setting for a story; but it also feels like the actual narrative isn't quite there: it feels like there's this opportunity for a great <a href="/scp-4205">SCP-4205</a> style story; where we see the Foundation struggling to contain this or w/e or someone affected by it slowly like, coming to terms with their imminent death; and it kinda feels like you have the angle for this but not necessarily the meat of the agents emotional arc?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

